       <h1
          class="text-5xl font-semibold text-center font-display text-blue-950 sm:text-6xl text-balance"
        >
          How is OTEP different?
        </h1>
<div class="mt-10 space-y-6 text-left text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
          <p>In a unit running a traditional assessment model, generally there will be a method for delivering content punctuated at intervals (usually 3-4 throughout semester) by assessment tasks, each of which comprises a portion of the total marks available for the unit.</p> 
            <img src="/public/MATTERImages/traditionalUnitStructureDiagram.png" />
            <p>Students achieve a grade by successfully accumulating the required number of marks for that grade over the course of the assessment schedule. Students cannot resubmit to improve on an attempt once they have received a mark for their work, meaning that all assessment submissions are high stakes events. Typically, unit learning outcomes are covered across the assessment pieces, and it is difficult to prove that students have successfully demonstrated competence in each learning outcome, as they may have been very good at one and very bad at another but still accumulated enough marks to pass.
</p><p>The defining feature of a MATTER designed unit is that the different grade achievement levels are represented by different tasks, which are rolled up into a portfolio of work that is a 100% assessment. Each task is evaluated by staff as either satisfactory and therefore complete, or not yet satisfactory. If not yet satisfactory, it is returned to the student with feedback that they can act upon before resubmitting. 
To achieve a Pass students must complete all Pass level tasks to the required standard. To achieve a Credit they must complete all the Pass level tasks AND all the Credit level tasks to the required standard. And so on. Once all tasks for a particular grade level have been marked as complete, the student is guaranteed at least that grade. The mark within that grade level is then determined by how well the student has engaged with the unit and with their own learning process.

          </p>
          <video controls autoplay src="/public/MATTERImages/TaskAssessmentGif.mp4" />
          <h2>OTEP Unit Structure</h2>
          <p>An OTEP unit is characterised by the series of tasks that comprise the assessment. Content delivery is flexible and can be designed in whatever way suits the unit. Some units are entirely flipped and self-paced, others are structured similarly to a traditional unit with weekly content delivery and small class activities</p>
         <img src="/public/MATTERImages/MATTERUnitStructure.png" />
